import{_ as e,o as a,c as o,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.CsGzzEsd.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Japanese Grammar Foundations","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Japanese Grammar Foundations"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"blogs/JLPT-N2/grammars.md","filePath":"blogs/JLPT-N2/grammars.md"}'),n={name:"blogs/JLPT-N2/grammars.md"};function s(i,t,h,d,l,c){return a(),o("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[r('<h1 id="japanese-grammar-foundations" tabindex="-1">Japanese Grammar Foundations <a class="header-anchor" href="#japanese-grammar-foundations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Japanese Grammar Foundations&quot;">​</a></h1><p>These basic patterns are useful to review before studying N2 grammar. In the patterns below, <strong>N</strong> means a noun, and <strong>A/B</strong> stand for words or phrases in the sentence.</p><h2 id="a-は-b-です" tabindex="-1">A は B です <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-は-b-です" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A は B です&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> は (pronounced <em>wa</em>) marks the topic: “as for A.” です makes a noun or な-adjective predicate polite. The topic is not always the grammatical subject.</p><ul><li>私は留学生です。 — I am an international student.</li><li>あの方は先生です。 — That person is a teacher.</li></ul><p><strong>Negative:</strong> A は B ではありません (or the conversational B じゃありません).</p><ul><li>私は先生ではありません。 — I am not a teacher.</li></ul><p><strong>Question:</strong> Add か to the end of a polite sentence. In writing, a question mark is optional.</p><ul><li>あの方は先生ですか。 — Is that person a teacher?</li></ul><p><strong>Connecting predicates:</strong> B で connects a noun or な-adjective predicate to another predicate. This で is a connective form of the copula, not the particle for the location of an action.</p><ul><li>田中さんは先生で、作家です。 — Tanaka is a teacher and a writer.</li></ul><h2 id="demonstratives" tabindex="-1">Demonstratives <a class="header-anchor" href="#demonstratives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Demonstratives&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The first syllable shows where the referent is relative to the speakers: <strong>こ</strong> near the speaker, <strong>そ</strong> near the listener or something already mentioned, <strong>あ</strong> away from both, and <strong>ど</strong> for a question. In conversation, そ and あ can also refer to familiar or remembered things; physical distance is only the starting point.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Thing</th><th>Place</th><th>Direction / polite person</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Near speaker</td><td>これ</td><td>ここ</td><td>こちら</td></tr><tr><td>Near listener</td><td>それ</td><td>そこ</td><td>そちら</td></tr><tr><td>Away from both</td><td>あれ</td><td>あそこ</td><td>あちら</td></tr><tr><td>Question</td><td>どれ</td><td>どこ</td><td>どちら</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>これは私の本です。 — This is my book.</li><li>駅はどちらですか。 — Which way is the station? (polite)</li></ul><p>こちら／そちら／あちら／どちら can also politely refer to a person or choice, depending on context.</p><h2 id="n1-に-n2-が-あります-います" tabindex="-1">N1 に N2 が あります／います <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-に-n2-が-あります-います" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N1 に N2 が あります／います&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> This pattern introduces N2 as existing at N1. に marks the location; が marks what exists. Use <strong>あります</strong> for things and plants, and <strong>います</strong> for people and animals.</p><ul><li>机の上に本があります。 — There is a book on the desk.</li><li>教室に学生がいます。 — There are students in the classroom.</li></ul><p>For possession, the location can be a person: 私には兄がいます。 — I have an older brother (literally, “As for me, an older brother exists”).</p><h2 id="n1-には-n2-が-あります-います" tabindex="-1">N1 には N2 が あります／います <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-には-n2-が-あります-います" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N1 には N2 が あります／います&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> には combines the location marker に with the topic or contrast marker は. It makes N1 the frame of the statement and can imply a contrast with other places or people. Use があります／います according to N2.</p><ul><li>この町には大きな公園があります。 — This town has a large park.</li><li>私のクラスには留学生がいます。 — There are international students in my class.</li></ul><p>Compare <strong>教室に学生がいます</strong> (“There are students in the classroom”) with <strong>教室には学生がいます</strong> (“In the classroom, there are students”), which puts more focus on the classroom.</p><h2 id="n1-は-n2-に-あります-います" tabindex="-1">N1 は N2 に あります／います <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-は-n2-に-あります-います" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N1 は N2 に あります／います&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Use this order when N1 is already known and you want to say <strong>where</strong> it is. は marks the known topic; に marks its location. Choose あります／います according to N1.</p><ul><li>本は机の上にあります。 — The book is on the desk.</li><li>先生は職員室にいます。 — The teacher is in the staff room.</li></ul><p>Compare <strong>机の上に本があります</strong> (“There is a book on the desk”), which introduces the book, with <strong>本は机の上にあります</strong> (“The book is on the desk”), which gives the location of a known book.</p><h2 id="past-tense-noun-predicates" tabindex="-1">Past tense: noun predicates <a class="header-anchor" href="#past-tense-noun-predicates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Past tense: noun predicates&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> For a polite noun sentence, change です to <strong>でした</strong>. The polite negative past is <strong>ではありませんでした</strong> (or conversational <strong>じゃありませんでした</strong>). These forms also work with な-adjective predicates.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Meaning</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Past affirmative</td><td>A は B でした</td><td>昨日は休みでした。 — Yesterday was a day off.</td></tr><tr><td>Past negative</td><td>A は B ではありませんでした</td><td>昨日は休みではありませんでした。 — Yesterday was not a day off.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The plain forms are <strong>だった</strong> and <strong>ではなかった</strong> (or <strong>じゃなかった</strong>): 昨日は休みだった。 — Yesterday was a day off.</p><h2 id="past-tense-verb-predicates" tabindex="-1">Past tense: verb predicates <a class="header-anchor" href="#past-tense-verb-predicates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Past tense: verb predicates&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> A polite verb changes from <strong>～ます</strong> to <strong>～ました</strong> in the affirmative past, and from <strong>～ません</strong> to <strong>～ませんでした</strong> in the negative past. The plain past form depends on the verb group, so it cannot be made by simply replacing ます with た.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Meaning</th><th>Polite</th><th>Plain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Past affirmative</td><td>昨日、映画を見ました。 — I watched a film yesterday.</td><td>昨日、映画を見た。</td></tr><tr><td>Past negative</td><td>昨日、映画を見ませんでした。 — I did not watch a film yesterday.</td><td>昨日、映画を見なかった。</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For example, 行きます → 行きました → 行った, while 食べます → 食べました → 食べた.</p><h2 id="question-words-with-が" tabindex="-1">Question words with が <a class="header-anchor" href="#question-words-with-が" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Question words with が&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> When a question word asks <strong>who or what the subject is</strong>, mark it with が: 誰が／何が. The answer commonly uses が to identify that subject. Use は instead if the answer treats the person or thing as an established topic or makes a contrast; “a が question must be answered with が” is too absolute.</p><ul><li>誰が来ましたか。 — Who came?</li><li>田中さんが来ました。 — Tanaka came.</li><li>何がありますか。 — What is there?</li><li>机の上に本があります。 — There is a book on the desk.</li></ul><h2 id="この-その-あの-どの" tabindex="-1">この／その／あの／どの <a class="header-anchor" href="#この-その-あの-どの" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;この／その／あの／どの&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> These are <strong>連体詞</strong> (words that modify a noun directly). They must be followed by a noun: この本 (“this book”), その本 (“that book near you / the book mentioned”), あの本 (“that book over there”), どの本 (“which book”). To refer to a thing without naming the noun, use これ／それ／あれ／どれ instead.</p><ul><li>この本は面白いです。 — This book is interesting.</li><li>これは面白いです。 — This is interesting.</li><li>どの本があなたのですか。 — Which book is yours?</li><li>どれがあなたのですか。 — Which one is yours?</li></ul>',42)])])}const m=e(n,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
